> Module Uc

# Universal Credit: skeleton rules for 2026/27

This file holds a thin slice of Universal Credit for a single claimant: the
standard allowance, the housing costs element, the work allowance and the
earnings deduction, and the award that follows from them. A second part,
"Universal Credit: nil assessment periods", follows the award across a short
window of periods in which earnings can take a period to a nil award. The file
models only what is needed to exercise the pipeline and the queries built on
it.

Simplifications, stated so that nobody reads more into the rules than is there:

* Only single claimants are modelled.
* The work allowance is applied when the claimant has limited capability for
  work (`has_lcw`). Responsibility for a child is not modelled.
* The housing costs element is an input, taken as already worked out. Rent,
  the Local Housing Allowance cap, the under-occupancy deduction, housing cost
  contributions and the other steps of regulation 26 and Schedule 4 are not
  modelled. See `docs/model-gaps.md`.
* The LCWRA element, the amount for responsibility for children and young
  persons and every other element are left out. The maximum amount is the
  standard allowance plus the housing costs element.
* Unearned income is not modelled.
* `earned_income` is employed earnings as regulation 55 defines them, not
  gross pay: earnings after deducting income tax, primary Class 1 National
  Insurance contributions, relievable pension contributions and payroll
  giving
  ([regulation 55(5)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/55),
  checked 29 October 2026).

Catala's English syntax writes money with the `$` symbol. In this file the unit
is pounds sterling.

Every parameter below is defined only for assessment periods starting on or
after 6 April 2026. For an earlier date no definition applies, and Catala stops
with an error instead of returning a value from rates that do not apply.
Sources were checked on 29 October 2026.

## Scope declaration

```catala-metadata
declaration scope UniversalCredit:
  input assessment_period_start content date
  input earned_income content money
  input aged_25_or_over content boolean
  input has_lcw content boolean
  input housing_costs_element content money
  internal deduction_rate content decimal
  output standard_allowance content money
  output work_allowance content money
  output earnings_deduction content money
  output maximum_amount content money
  output award content money
```

## Standard allowance

Regulation 36 gives the amount of the standard allowance. The rates below were
substituted by [S.I. 2026/113](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/113),
with effect for assessment periods starting on or after 6 April 2026.

"single claimant aged under 25": £338.58, in the amount column
([regulation 36](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/36),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition standard_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06| and not aged_25_or_over
    consequence equals $338.58
```

"single claimant aged 25 or over": £424.90, in the amount column
([regulation 36](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/36),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition standard_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06| and aged_25_or_over
    consequence equals $424.90
```

## Housing costs element

The housing costs element is an input. It stands for the amount that
[regulation 26](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/26) and
Schedule 4 of the Universal Credit Regulations 2013 give for eligible rent,
taken as already worked out, per assessment period (checked 29 October 2026).
Nothing that sits between the rent and the element is modelled. The gap is
recorded in `docs/model-gaps.md`.

## Maximum amount

Section 8(2) of the
[Welfare Reform Act 2012](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/5/section/8)
makes the maximum amount the total of any amounts included for the standard
allowance, responsibility for children and young persons, housing costs, and
other particular needs or circumstances (checked 29 October 2026). Only the
first and third are modelled, and the third is an input.

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition maximum_amount equals standard_allowance + housing_costs_element
```

## Work allowance

Regulation 22(2) gives the amount of the work allowance. Both amounts were
substituted by [S.I. 2026/148](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/148)
in the version of the regulation dated 6 April 2026. The higher amount applies
when the award has no housing costs element, and the lower one when it has.
This file reads "contains an amount for the housing costs element" as an
element above zero, as PolicyEngine UK does. The reading is the project's, for
specialist review.

The three definitions below are parallel cases, not a rule with exceptions, so
their conditions exclude each other.

Single claimant who "has limited capability for work", where "the award
contains no amount for the housing costs element": £710.00, the higher work
allowance
([regulation 22(2)(a)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition work_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06|
      and has_lcw and not (housing_costs_element > $0.00)
    consequence equals $710.00
```

Single claimant who "has limited capability for work", where "the award does
contain an amount for the housing costs element": £427.00, the lower work
allowance
([regulation 22(2)(b)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition work_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06|
      and has_lcw and housing_costs_element > $0.00
    consequence equals $427.00
```

Regulation 22(1)(b)(i) covers a claimant for whom no work allowance is
specified. The whole of that claimant's earned income is then used, which this
file represents as a work allowance of zero, whether or not the award has a
housing costs element.

"in a case where no work allowance is specified in the table below"
([regulation 22](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition work_allowance
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06| and not has_lcw
    consequence equals $0.00
```

## Earnings deduction

The deduction rate is 55%, as regulation 22(1)(b) reads in the version dated
6 April 2026. The figure was substituted for 63% by regulation 2(1) of
[S.I. 2021/1283](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2021/1283), in force from
24 November 2021 (checked 29 October 2026).

"55% of the amount by which that earned income exceeds the work allowance"
([regulation 22](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/22),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition deduction_rate
    under condition assessment_period_start >= |2026-04-06|
    consequence equals 55%
```

The deduction is one money-times-decimal step, so Catala rounds it once, to the
nearest penny with halves rounded away from zero. That matches regulation 6(1).

"otherwise it is to be treated as a penny"
([regulation 6](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/6),
checked 29 October 2026)

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition earnings_deduction equals
    if earned_income > work_allowance
    then (earned_income - work_allowance) * deduction_rate
    else $0.00
```

## Award

Regulation 22(1) deducts earned and unearned income from the maximum amount
under [section 8(3) of the Welfare Reform Act 2012](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/5/section/8). The award cannot be negative.

```catala
scope UniversalCredit:
  definition award equals
    if maximum_amount - earnings_deduction > $0.00
    then maximum_amount - earnings_deduction
    else $0.00
```

# Universal Credit: nil assessment periods

The rules above work out one assessment period. The rules below follow an
award across a short window of consecutive monthly assessment periods, for
the case where earnings take a period to a nil award: the award ends, later
months can be treated as claims, and surplus earnings from the nil period are
counted against those claims.

`WindowStep` works out one period, given the state that the previous period
left. `ClaimWindow` chains three steps. `ClaimWindow4`, `ClaimWindow5` and
`ClaimWindow6` chain four, five and six, for measuring solver time only.

Every reading of the law in this part is the project's own, made from the
cited sources, and is for specialist review. The research behind it, with the
confidence of each reading, is in `docs/nil-months.md`, and the design is in
`docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-29-nil-assessment-periods-design.md`. Sources
were checked on 29 October 2026.

Three points are left to DWP's discretion, or rest on DWP's guidance rather
than on the regulations. Each is an input, so that every case can be asked
about:

* `claim_after_end`: whether claims are treated as made, or a fresh claim is
  made, in the months after the award ends.
* `claim_each_month`: whether a month in which that claim fails still counts
  as a claim.
* `lcw_carries_over`: whether the limited capability for work finding carries
  over to a new award.

Simplifications, in addition to those of the rules above:

* The state carried from one period to the next is whether the award is live,
  the surplus carried in, and how many months have passed since the award
  ended. Nothing else carries over: each period's award is worked out afresh.
* A fresh claim within 6 months of the end is treated in the same way as a
  claim treated as made under regulation 32A.
* Regulation 32A's extension for a claimant on a funded employment scheme is
  not modelled.
* Regulation 54A(5), under which no surplus is taken into account for a
  claimant who has "recently been a victim of domestic violence", is not
  represented.
* The thresholds use the maximum amount and the work allowance of the period
  being decided. The regulation does not say which period's figures apply.

## Step scope declaration

`live` means that the award continues into this period. `months_since_end` is
0 in a live award, and otherwise the number of months since the nil period
that ended it: 1 for the month after the nil period, and so on. `surplus` is
the surplus earnings carried in.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope WindowStep:
  input live content boolean
  input surplus content money
  input months_since_end content integer
  input start content date
  input earnings content money
  input aged_25_or_over content boolean
  input housing_costs_element content money
  input has_lcw content boolean
  input claim_after_end content boolean
  input claim_each_month content boolean
  input lcw_carries_over content boolean
  uc scope UniversalCredit
  internal claim_made content boolean
  internal lcw_used content boolean
  output earnings_used content money
  output award content money
  output entitled content boolean
  output nil_uc_threshold content money
  output relevant_threshold content money
  output live_next content boolean
  output surplus_next content money
  output months_since_end_next content integer
```

## Claims after the award ends

Under regulation 32A of the Claims and Payments Regulations 2013, where
entitlement to an award ceases because the income condition is not met, the
Secretary of State "may", "subject to any conditions the Secretary of State
considers appropriate", treat the claimant as making a claim on the first day
of each later month that would have been an assessment period, "up to a
maximum of 5"
([regulation 32A](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/380/regulation/32A),
checked 29 October 2026). The old assessment period dates are kept.

This file reads the 5 months as counted from the nil period, so that a claim
that also fails does not start a fresh run of 5. That follows ADM A2050,
Examples 2 and 3 (DWP's
[*Advice for Decision Making*](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/advice-for-decision-making-staff-guide),
chapter A2, checked 29 October 2026). The reading is the project's, for
specialist review.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition claim_made equals
    not live and claim_after_end
    and months_since_end >= 1 and months_since_end <= 5
```

## The limited capability for work finding

A claimant has limited capability for work if "it has been determined" that
they have it
([regulation 39](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/39),
checked 29 October 2026). The regulation sets no time limit tied to the award.
That the finding carries over to a new award within 6 months rests on DWP's
"Re-claims" staff guide, which says a determination "remains valid" if the
condition has not changed and the review date has not passed (V11.0,
[DEP2024-0442](https://data.parliament.uk/DepositedPapers/Files/DEP2024-0442/138._Reclaims_V11.0.pdf),
checked 29 October 2026). After the end, the finding is used only when
`lcw_carries_over` holds.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition lcw_used equals
    if live then has_lcw else has_lcw and lcw_carries_over
```

## Earnings used

Where regulation 54A applies to a claim, surplus earnings "are to be treated
as earned income" both for deciding whether there is entitlement and for
calculating the award
([regulation 54A(2)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/54A),
checked 29 October 2026). This file adds the surplus carried in to the
earnings of the period of each claim made after the end. In a live award, and
in a month with no claim, the earnings used are the period's own.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition earnings_used equals
    if claim_made then earnings + surplus else earnings
```

## The period's figures

The period's award, maximum amount and work allowance come from the rules
above, with the earnings used and the limited capability for work finding
used.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition uc.assessment_period_start equals start
  definition uc.earned_income equals earnings_used
  definition uc.aged_25_or_over equals aged_25_or_over
  definition uc.has_lcw equals lcw_used
  definition uc.housing_costs_element equals housing_costs_element
```

## The award and entitlement

Entitlement needs an amount payable of at least a prescribed minimum, and for
Universal Credit "the minimum is one penny"
([regulation 17](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/17);
Welfare Reform Act 2012,
[section 5](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2012/5/section/5); both
checked 29 October 2026). So a period whose award is below 1p ends the award.
The decision that ends it takes effect "from the first day of the assessment
period in which that change occurred"
([Decisions and Appeals Regulations 2013, Schedule 1](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/381/schedule/1),
paragraph 20, checked 29 October 2026), so nothing is paid for the nil period.
That the end is a supersession under paragraph 20 is the project's reading,
which ADM A2050, Example 3, supports.

Entitlement is tested on the rounded award itself, not on the nil UC threshold
below, because the threshold is where the unrounded award reaches zero, about
a penny above the point at which the rounded award falls below 1p.

The award is paid in a live award or in a month in which a claim is made, and
is nil otherwise.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition award equals
    if live or claim_made then uc.award else $0.00

  definition entitled equals award >= $0.01
```

## The nil UC threshold

Regulation 54A(6) defines "the nil UC threshold" as the amount of total earned
income "above which there would be no entitlement", by a formula in M, "the
maximum amount of an award of universal credit", U, "unearned income", and
WA, "the work allowance"
([regulation 54A(6)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/54A),
checked 29 October 2026). The formula is (M - U) / 55 x 100 + WA, with the work
allowance added unscaled, as ADM H3308 shows. Unearned income is not modelled,
so U is 0.

The formula does not say how to round. This file divides M by 55%, which is
M x 100/55 exactly, and rounds the result once to the penny under regulation 6,
as the ADM's worked figure is rounded (chapter H3, Appendix 2, Example 1). It
then adds the work allowance.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition nil_uc_threshold equals
    uc.maximum_amount / 0.55 + uc.work_allowance
```

## The relevant threshold

Regulation 54A(6) makes "the relevant threshold" "the nil UC threshold plus
£300"
([regulation 54A(6)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/54A),
checked 29 October 2026). Regulation 5 of S.I. 2015/345 substitutes £2,500 for
a claim "in respect of a period that begins before the end of the temporary de
minimis period"
([regulation 5](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2015/345/regulation/5),
checked 29 October 2026). The Secretary of State's
[determination](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/secretary-of-state-determination-under-regulation-5-of-the-universal-credit-surpluses-and-self-employed-losses-digital-service-amendment-regulatio--2/secretary-of-state-determination-under-regulation-5-of-the-universal-credit-surpluses-and-self-employed-losses-digital-service-amendment-regulatio),
made 20 January 2026, says that period "will end on 31st March 2027" (checked
29 October 2026).

This file applies £2,500 to a period beginning on or before 31 March 2027.
That a period beginning on 31 March 2027 itself is covered is the project's
inference, recorded in `docs/model-gaps.md`. The two definitions are parallel
cases, so their conditions exclude each other.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition relevant_threshold
    under condition start <= |2027-03-31|
    consequence equals nil_uc_threshold + $2,500.00

  definition relevant_threshold
    under condition start > |2027-03-31|
    consequence equals nil_uc_threshold + $300.00
```

## Surplus earnings carried on

Regulation 54A applies to a claim where the claimant had an "old award" that
terminated within the 6 months ending on the first day the claim is for, has
not been entitled since, and had total earned income "in the month that would
have been the final assessment period for the old award, had it not
terminated" above the relevant threshold
([regulation 54A(1)](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2013/376/regulation/54A),
checked 29 October 2026). Under paragraph (3), the surplus for the first claim
is that excess, "the original surplus". For each later claim, up to the fifth,
it is the amount, if any, by which the previous surplus plus the earnings in
the first assessment period of the previous claim "exceeded the relevant
threshold".

The surplus carried to the next period is:

* nil if this period is entitled, because a surplus is used up once an award
  is made and nothing carries into later periods of a new award (ADM H3,
  Appendix 2, Example 1);
* the original surplus, the excess of the earnings over the relevant
  threshold, if this period ended a live award;
* after the end, where a claim was made and failed, the excess of the earnings
  used over the relevant threshold if `claim_each_month` holds, and the surplus
  carried in, unchanged, if it does not (ADM A2050, Examples 3 and 4, differ on
  whether a failed month is a claim);
* after the end, with no claim, the surplus carried in, unchanged, because
  paragraph (3) counts claims, not months.

No surplus is defined for a sixth claim. The 5-month limit on claims above
means that none is made.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition surplus_next equals
    if entitled then $0.00
    else if live then
      (if earnings > relevant_threshold
       then earnings - relevant_threshold
       else $0.00)
    else if claim_made and claim_each_month then
      (if earnings_used > relevant_threshold
       then earnings_used - relevant_threshold
       else $0.00)
    else surplus
```

## The state carried to the next period

The award continues into the next period if this period is entitled. A new
award after the end restarts the state, so a later nil period ends the award
again and starts a fresh run of claims and a fresh surplus.

```catala
scope WindowStep:
  definition live_next equals entitled

  definition months_since_end_next equals
    if entitled then 0
    else if live then 1
    else months_since_end + 1
```

## The window of three periods

`ClaimWindow` chains three steps. The window starts in a live award, with no
surplus carried in. Each later step takes the state that the previous step
left. The household facts and the three judgement points are the same in
every period. The start dates are inputs, one calendar month apart, worked out
by the caller.

`surplus_after_<k>` is the surplus that step k carries to the next period, and
`total_award` is the sum of the awards.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope ClaimWindow:
  input start_1 content date
  input start_2 content date
  input start_3 content date
  input earnings_1 content money
  input earnings_2 content money
  input earnings_3 content money
  input aged_25_or_over content boolean
  input housing_costs_element content money
  input has_lcw content boolean
  input claim_after_end content boolean
  input claim_each_month content boolean
  input lcw_carries_over content boolean
  step_1 scope WindowStep
  step_2 scope WindowStep
  step_3 scope WindowStep
  output award_1 content money
  output earnings_used_1 content money
  output entitled_1 content boolean
  output surplus_after_1 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_1 content money
  output relevant_threshold_1 content money
  output award_2 content money
  output earnings_used_2 content money
  output entitled_2 content boolean
  output surplus_after_2 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_2 content money
  output relevant_threshold_2 content money
  output award_3 content money
  output earnings_used_3 content money
  output entitled_3 content boolean
  output surplus_after_3 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_3 content money
  output relevant_threshold_3 content money
  output total_award content money
```

```catala
scope ClaimWindow:
  definition step_1.live equals true
  definition step_1.surplus equals $0.00
  definition step_1.months_since_end equals 0
  definition step_1.start equals start_1
  definition step_1.earnings equals earnings_1
  definition step_1.aged_25_or_over equals aged_25_or_over
  definition step_1.housing_costs_element equals housing_costs_element
  definition step_1.has_lcw equals has_lcw
  definition step_1.claim_after_end equals claim_after_end
  definition step_1.claim_each_month equals claim_each_month
  definition step_1.lcw_carries_over equals lcw_carries_over
  definition step_2.live equals step_1.live_next
  definition step_2.surplus equals step_1.surplus_next
  definition step_2.months_since_end equals step_1.months_since_end_next
  definition step_2.start equals start_2
  definition step_2.earnings equals earnings_2
  definition step_2.aged_25_or_over equals aged_25_or_over
  definition step_2.housing_costs_element equals housing_costs_element
  definition step_2.has_lcw equals has_lcw
  definition step_2.claim_after_end equals claim_after_end
  definition step_2.claim_each_month equals claim_each_month
  definition step_2.lcw_carries_over equals lcw_carries_over
  definition step_3.live equals step_2.live_next
  definition step_3.surplus equals step_2.surplus_next
  definition step_3.months_since_end equals step_2.months_since_end_next
  definition step_3.start equals start_3
  definition step_3.earnings equals earnings_3
  definition step_3.aged_25_or_over equals aged_25_or_over
  definition step_3.housing_costs_element equals housing_costs_element
  definition step_3.has_lcw equals has_lcw
  definition step_3.claim_after_end equals claim_after_end
  definition step_3.claim_each_month equals claim_each_month
  definition step_3.lcw_carries_over equals lcw_carries_over
  definition award_1 equals step_1.award
  definition earnings_used_1 equals step_1.earnings_used
  definition entitled_1 equals step_1.entitled
  definition surplus_after_1 equals step_1.surplus_next
  definition nil_uc_threshold_1 equals step_1.nil_uc_threshold
  definition relevant_threshold_1 equals step_1.relevant_threshold
  definition award_2 equals step_2.award
  definition earnings_used_2 equals step_2.earnings_used
  definition entitled_2 equals step_2.entitled
  definition surplus_after_2 equals step_2.surplus_next
  definition nil_uc_threshold_2 equals step_2.nil_uc_threshold
  definition relevant_threshold_2 equals step_2.relevant_threshold
  definition award_3 equals step_3.award
  definition earnings_used_3 equals step_3.earnings_used
  definition entitled_3 equals step_3.entitled
  definition surplus_after_3 equals step_3.surplus_next
  definition nil_uc_threshold_3 equals step_3.nil_uc_threshold
  definition relevant_threshold_3 equals step_3.relevant_threshold
  definition total_award equals
    award_1 + award_2 + award_3
```

## Longer windows, for measuring solver time

`ClaimWindow4`, `ClaimWindow5` and `ClaimWindow6` follow the same pattern as
`ClaimWindow` with four, five and six steps. They use the same `WindowStep`
and add no rules. They exist to measure how solver time grows with the length
of the window, and no finding uses them.

### Four periods

```catala-metadata
declaration scope ClaimWindow4:
  input start_1 content date
  input start_2 content date
  input start_3 content date
  input start_4 content date
  input earnings_1 content money
  input earnings_2 content money
  input earnings_3 content money
  input earnings_4 content money
  input aged_25_or_over content boolean
  input housing_costs_element content money
  input has_lcw content boolean
  input claim_after_end content boolean
  input claim_each_month content boolean
  input lcw_carries_over content boolean
  step_1 scope WindowStep
  step_2 scope WindowStep
  step_3 scope WindowStep
  step_4 scope WindowStep
  output award_1 content money
  output earnings_used_1 content money
  output entitled_1 content boolean
  output surplus_after_1 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_1 content money
  output relevant_threshold_1 content money
  output award_2 content money
  output earnings_used_2 content money
  output entitled_2 content boolean
  output surplus_after_2 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_2 content money
  output relevant_threshold_2 content money
  output award_3 content money
  output earnings_used_3 content money
  output entitled_3 content boolean
  output surplus_after_3 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_3 content money
  output relevant_threshold_3 content money
  output award_4 content money
  output earnings_used_4 content money
  output entitled_4 content boolean
  output surplus_after_4 content money
  output nil_uc_threshold_4 content money
  output relevant_threshold_4 content money
  output total_award content money
```

```catala
scope ClaimWindow4:
  definition step_1.live equals true
  definition step_1.surplus equals $0.00
  definition step_1.months_since_end equals 0
  definition step_1.start equals start_1
  definition step_1.earnings equals earnings_1
  definition step_1.aged_25_or_over equals aged_25_or_over
  definition step_1.housing_costs_element equals housing_costs_element
  definition step_1.has_lcw equals has_lcw
  definition step_1.claim_after_end equals claim_after_end
  definition step_1.claim_each_month equals claim_each_month
  definition step_1.lcw_carries_over equals lcw_carries_over
  definition step_2.live equals step_1.live_next
  definition step_2.surplus equals step_1.surplus_next
